#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:5
msgid "Package Discovery and Namespace Packages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:8
msgid ""
"a full specification for the keywords supplied to ``setup.cfg`` or ``setup."
"py`` can be found at :doc:`keywords reference </references/keywords>`"
msgstr ""
"``setup.cfg`` 或 ``setup.py`` 中提供的關鍵字的完整規格可以在\\\\ :doc:`關鍵字"
"參考 </references/keywords>`\\\\ 中找到"

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:12
msgid ""
"The examples provided here are only to demonstrate the functionality "
"introduced. More metadata and options arguments need to be supplied if you "
"want to replicate them on your system. If you are completely new to "
"setuptools, the :doc:`quickstart` section is a good place to start."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:17
msgid ""
"``Setuptools`` provides powerful tools to handle package discovery, "
"including support for namespace packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:20
msgid ""
"Normally, you would specify the packages to be included manually in the "
"following manner:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:24
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"#...\n"
"packages =\n"
"    mypkg\n"
"    mypkg.subpkg1\n"
"    mypkg.subpkg2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:35
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    # ...\n"
"    packages=['mypkg', 'mypkg.subpkg1', 'mypkg.subpkg2']\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:44
msgid ""
"# ...\n"
"[tool.setuptools]\n"
"packages = [\"mypkg\", \"mypkg.subpkg1\", \"mypkg.subpkg2\"]\n"
"# ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:52
msgid ""
"If your packages are not in the root of the repository or do not correspond "
"exactly to the directory structure (that is, the directory layout in your "
"repository does not match the dotted package hierarchy you want installed on "
"the end-user's machine), you also need to configure ``package_dir``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:59
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"# ...\n"
"package_dir =\n"
"    = src\n"
"    # directory containing all the packages (e.g.  src/mypkg, src/mypkg/"
"subpkg1, ...)\n"
"# OR\n"
"package_dir =\n"
"    mypkg = lib\n"
"    # mypkg.module corresponds to lib/module.py\n"
"    mypkg.subpkg1 = lib1\n"
"    # mypkg.subpkg1.module1 corresponds to lib1/module1.py\n"
"    mypkg.subpkg2 = lib2\n"
"    # mypkg.subpkg2.module2 corresponds to lib2/module2.py\n"
"# ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:78
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    # ...\n"
"    package_dir = {\"\": \"src\"}\n"
"    # directory containing all the packages (e.g.  src/mypkg, src/mypkg/"
"subpkg1, ...)\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"# OR\n"
"\n"
"setup(\n"
"    # ...\n"
"    package_dir = {\n"
"        \"mypkg\": \"lib\",  # mypkg.module corresponds to lib/module.py\n"
"        \"mypkg.subpkg1\": \"lib1\",  # mypkg.subpkg1.module1 corresponds to "
"lib1/module1.py\n"
"        \"mypkg.subpkg2\": \"lib2\",  # mypkg.subpkg2.module2 corresponds to "
"lib2/module2.py\n"
"        # ...\n"
"    }\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:100
msgid ""
"[tool.setuptools]\n"
"# ...\n"
"package-dir = {\"\" = \"src\"}\n"
"    # directory containing all the packages (e.g.  src/mypkg1, src/mypkg2)\n"
"\n"
"# OR\n"
"\n"
"[tool.setuptools.package-dir]\n"
"mypkg = \"lib\"\n"
"# mypkg.module corresponds to lib/module.py\n"
"\"mypkg.subpkg1\" = \"lib1\"\n"
"# mypkg.subpkg1.module1 corresponds to lib1/module1.py\n"
"\"mypkg.subpkg2\" = \"lib2\"\n"
"# mypkg.subpkg2.module2 corresponds to lib2/module2.py\n"
"# ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:118
msgid ""
"This can get tiresome really quickly. To speed things up, you can rely on "
"setuptools automatic discovery, or use the provided tools, as explained in "
"the following sections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:123
msgid ""
"Although ``setuptools`` allows developers to create a very complex mapping "
"between directory names and package names, it is better to *keep it simple* "
"and reflect the desired package hierarchy in the directory structure, "
"preserving the same names."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:131
msgid "Automatic discovery"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:133
msgid ""
"By default ``setuptools`` will consider 2 popular project layouts, each one "
"with its own set of advantages and disadvantages [#layout1]_ [#layout2]_ as "
"discussed in the following sections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:137
msgid ""
"Setuptools will automatically scan your project directory looking for these "
"layouts and try to guess the correct values for the :ref:`packages "
"<declarative config>` and :doc:`py_modules </references/keywords>` "
"configuration."
msgstr ""
"Setuptools 會自動掃描您的專案目錄，尋找這些配置，並嘗試猜測\\ :ref:`軟體包 "
"<declarative config>` 和 :doc:`py_modules </references/keywords>`\\ 組態。"

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:142
msgid ""
"Automatic discovery will **only** be enabled if you **don't** provide any "
"configuration for ``packages`` and ``py_modules``. If at least one of them "
"is explicitly set, automatic discovery will not take place."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:146
msgid ""
"**Note**: specifying ``ext_modules`` might also prevent auto-discover from "
"taking place, unless your opt into :doc:`pyproject_config` (which will "
"disable the backward compatible behaviour)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:153
msgid "src-layout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:154
msgid ""
"The project should contain a ``src`` directory under the project root and "
"all modules and packages meant for distribution are placed inside this "
"directory::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:158
msgid ""
"project_root_directory\n"
"├── pyproject.toml  # AND/OR setup.cfg, setup.py\n"
"├── ...\n"
"└── src/\n"
"    └── mypkg/\n"
"        ├── __init__.py\n"
"        ├── ...\n"
"        ├── module.py\n"
"        ├── subpkg1/\n"
"        │   ├── __init__.py\n"
"        │   ├── ...\n"
"        │   └── module1.py\n"
"        └── subpkg2/\n"
"            ├── __init__.py\n"
"            ├── ...\n"
"            └── module2.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:175
msgid ""
"This layout is very handy when you wish to use automatic discovery, since "
"you don't have to worry about other Python files or folders in your project "
"root being distributed by mistake. In some circumstances it can be also less "
"error-prone for testing or when using :pep:`420`-style packages. On the "
"other hand you cannot rely on the implicit ``PYTHONPATH=.`` to fire up the "
"Python REPL and play with your package (you will need an `editable install`_ "
"to be able to do that)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:186
msgid "flat-layout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:187
msgid "*(also known as \"adhoc\")*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:189
msgid "The package folder(s) are placed directly under the project root::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:191
msgid ""
"project_root_directory\n"
"├── pyproject.toml  # AND/OR setup.cfg, setup.py\n"
"├── ...\n"
"└── mypkg/\n"
"    ├── __init__.py\n"
"    ├── ...\n"
"    ├── module.py\n"
"    ├── subpkg1/\n"
"    │   ├── __init__.py\n"
"    │   ├── ...\n"
"    │   └── module1.py\n"
"    └── subpkg2/\n"
"        ├── __init__.py\n"
"        ├── ...\n"
"        └── module2.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:207
msgid ""
"This layout is very practical for using the REPL, but in some situations it "
"can be more error-prone (e.g. during tests or if you have a bunch of folders "
"or Python files hanging around your project root)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:211
msgid ""
"To avoid confusion, file and folder names that are used by popular tools (or "
"that correspond to well-known conventions, such as distributing "
"documentation alongside the project code) are automatically filtered out in "
"the case of *flat-layout*:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/discovery.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.discovery.FlatLayoutPackageFinder.DEFAULT_EXCLUDE:1
msgid "Reserved package names"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/setuptools/discovery.py:docstring
#: of setuptools.discovery.FlatLayoutModuleFinder.DEFAULT_EXCLUDE:1
msgid "Reserved top-level module names"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:221
msgid ""
"If you are using auto-discovery with *flat-layout*, ``setuptools`` will "
"refuse to create :term:`distribution archives <Distribution Package>` with "
"multiple top-level packages or modules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:225
msgid ""
"This is done to prevent common errors such as accidentally publishing code "
"not meant for distribution (e.g. maintenance-related scripts)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:228
msgid ""
"Users that purposefully want to create multi-package distributions are "
"advised to use :ref:`custom-discovery` or the ``src-layout``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:231
msgid ""
"There is also a handy variation of the *flat-layout* for utilities/libraries "
"that can be implemented with a single Python file:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:235
msgid "single-module distribution"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:237
msgid ""
"A standalone module is placed directly under the project root, instead of "
"inside a package folder::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:240
msgid ""
"project_root_directory\n"
"├── pyproject.toml  # AND/OR setup.cfg, setup.py\n"
"├── ...\n"
"└── single_file_lib.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:249
msgid "Custom discovery"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:251
msgid ""
"If the automatic discovery does not work for you (e.g., you want to "
"*include* in the distribution top-level packages with reserved names such as "
"``tasks``, ``example`` or ``docs``, or you want to *exclude* nested packages "
"that would be otherwise included), you can use the provided tools for "
"package discovery:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:259
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"packages = find:\n"
"#or\n"
"packages = find_namespace:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:268
msgid ""
"from setuptools import find_packages\n"
"# or\n"
"from setuptools import find_namespace_packages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:276
msgid ""
"# ...\n"
"[tool.setuptools.packages]\n"
"find = {}  # Scanning implicit namespaces is active by default\n"
"# OR\n"
"find = {namespaces = false}  # Disable implicit namespaces"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:286
msgid "Finding simple packages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:287
msgid ""
"Let's start with the first tool. ``find:`` (``find_packages()``) takes a "
"source directory and two lists of package name patterns to exclude and "
"include, and then returns a list of ``str`` representing the packages it "
"could find. To use it, consider the following directory::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:292
msgid ""
"mypkg\n"
"├── pyproject.toml  # AND/OR setup.cfg, setup.py\n"
"└── src\n"
"    ├── pkg1\n"
"    │   └── __init__.py\n"
"    ├── pkg2\n"
"    │   └── __init__.py\n"
"    ├── additional\n"
"    │   └── __init__.py\n"
"    └── pkg\n"
"        └── namespace\n"
"            └── __init__.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:305
msgid ""
"To have setuptools to automatically include packages found in ``src`` that "
"start with the name ``pkg`` and not ``additional``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:310
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"packages = find:\n"
"package_dir =\n"
"    =src\n"
"\n"
"[options.packages.find]\n"
"where = src\n"
"include = pkg*\n"
"# alternatively: `exclude = additional*`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:323
msgid ""
"``pkg`` does not contain an ``__init__.py`` file, therefore ``pkg."
"namespace`` is ignored by ``find:`` (see ``find_namespace:`` below)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:328
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    # ...\n"
"    packages=find_packages(\n"
"        where='src',\n"
"        include=['pkg*'],  # alternatively: `exclude=['additional*']`\n"
"    ),\n"
"    package_dir={\"\": \"src\"}\n"
"    # ...\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:342
msgid ""
"``pkg`` does not contain an ``__init__.py`` file, therefore ``pkg."
"namespace`` is ignored by ``find_packages()`` (see "
"``find_namespace_packages()`` below)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:348
msgid ""
"[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\n"
"where = [\"src\"]\n"
"include = [\"pkg*\"]  # alternatively: `exclude = [\"additional*\"]`\n"
"namespaces = false"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:356
msgid ""
"When using ``tool.setuptools.packages.find`` in ``pyproject.toml``, "
"setuptools will consider :pep:`implicit namespaces <420>` by default when "
"scanning your project directory. To avoid ``pkg.namespace`` from being added "
"to your package list you can set ``namespaces = false``. This will prevent "
"any folder without an ``__init__.py`` file from being scanned."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:364
msgid ""
"``include`` and ``exclude`` accept strings representing :mod:`glob` "
"patterns. These patterns should match the **full** name of the Python module "
"(as if it was written in an ``import`` statement)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:368
msgid ""
"For example if you have ``util`` pattern, it will match ``util/__init__.py`` "
"but not ``util/files/__init__.py``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:371
msgid ""
"The fact that the parent package is matched by the pattern will not dictate "
"if the submodule will be included or excluded from the distribution. You "
"will need to explicitly add a wildcard (e.g. ``util*``) if you want the "
"pattern to also match submodules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:379
msgid "Finding namespace packages"
msgstr "尋找命名空間包"

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:380
msgid ""
"``setuptools``  provides ``find_namespace:`` (``find_namespace_packages()``) "
"which behaves similarly to ``find:`` but works with namespace packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:383
msgid ""
"Before diving in, it is important to have a good understanding of what :pep:"
"`namespace packages <420>` are. Here is a quick recap."
msgstr ""
"在深入探討之前，充分理解\\ :pep:`命名空間包 <420>`\\ 的概念至關重要。以下是快"
"速回顧。"

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:386
msgid "When you have two packages organized as follows:"
msgstr "當您有兩個軟體包且組織結構如下："

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:388
msgid ""
"/Users/Desktop/timmins/foo/__init__.py\n"
"/Library/timmins/bar/__init__.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:393
msgid ""
"If both ``Desktop`` and ``Library`` are on your ``PYTHONPATH``, then a "
"namespace package called ``timmins`` will be created automatically for you "
"when you invoke the import mechanism, allowing you to accomplish the "
"following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:397
msgid ""
">>> import timmins.foo\n"
">>> import timmins.bar"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:402
msgid ""
"as if there is only one ``timmins`` on your system. The two packages can "
"then be distributed separately and installed individually without affecting "
"the other one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:406
msgid ""
"Now, suppose you decide to package the ``foo`` part for distribution and "
"start by creating a project directory organized as follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:409
msgid ""
"foo\n"
"├── pyproject.toml  # AND/OR setup.cfg, setup.py\n"
"└── src\n"
"    └── timmins\n"
"        └── foo\n"
"            └── __init__.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:416
msgid ""
"If you want the ``timmins.foo`` to be automatically included in the "
"distribution, then you will need to specify:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:421
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"package_dir =\n"
"    =src\n"
"packages = find_namespace:\n"
"\n"
"[options.packages.find]\n"
"where = src"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:431
msgid ""
"``find:`` won't work because ``timmins`` doesn't contain ``__init__.py`` "
"directly, instead, you have to use ``find_namespace:``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:434
msgid ""
"You can think of ``find_namespace:`` as identical to ``find:`` except it "
"would count a directory as a package even if it doesn't contain ``__init__."
"py`` file directly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:440
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    # ...\n"
"    packages=find_namespace_packages(where='src'),\n"
"    package_dir={\"\": \"src\"}\n"
"    # ...\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:449
msgid ""
"When you use ``find_packages()``, all directories without an ``__init__.py`` "
"file will be ignored. On the other hand, ``find_namespace_packages()`` will "
"scan all directories."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:456
msgid ""
"[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\n"
"where = [\"src\"]"
msgstr ""
"[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\n"
"where = [\"src\"]"

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:461
msgid ""
"When using ``tool.setuptools.packages.find`` in ``pyproject.toml``, "
"setuptools will consider :pep:`implicit namespaces <420>` by default when "
"scanning your project directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:465
msgid ""
"After installing the package distribution, ``timmins.foo`` would become "
"available to your interpreter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:469
msgid ""
"Please have in mind that ``find_namespace:`` (setup.cfg), "
"``find_namespace_packages()`` (setup.py) and ``find`` (pyproject.toml) will "
"scan **all** folders that you have in your project directory if you use a :"
"ref:`flat-layout`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:474
msgid ""
"If used naïvely, this might result in unwanted files being added to your "
"final wheel. For example, with a project directory organized as follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:477
msgid ""
"foo\n"
"├── docs\n"
"│   └── conf.py\n"
"├── timmins\n"
"│   └── foo\n"
"│       └── __init__.py\n"
"└── tests\n"
"    └── tests_foo\n"
"        └── __init__.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:487
msgid ""
"final users will end up installing not only ``timmins.foo``, but also "
"``docs`` and ``tests.tests_foo``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:490
msgid ""
"A simple way to fix this is to adopt the aforementioned :ref:`src-layout`, "
"or make sure to properly configure the ``include`` and/or ``exclude`` "
"accordingly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:495
msgid ""
"After :ref:`building your package <building>`, you can have a look if all "
"the files are correct (nothing missing or extra), by running the following "
"commands:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:499
msgid ""
"tar tf dist/*.tar.gz\n"
"unzip -l dist/*.whl"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:504
msgid ""
"This requires the ``tar`` and ``unzip`` to be installed in your OS. On "
"Windows you can also use a GUI program such as 7zip_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:509
msgid "Legacy Namespace Packages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:510
msgid ""
"The fact you can create namespace packages so effortlessly above is credited "
"to :pep:`420`. It used to be more cumbersome to accomplish the same result. "
"Historically, there were two methods to create namespace packages. One is "
"the ``pkg_resources`` style supported by ``setuptools`` and the other one "
"being ``pkgutils`` style offered by ``pkgutils`` module in Python. Both are "
"now considered *deprecated* despite the fact they still linger in many "
"existing packages. These two differ in many subtle yet significant aspects "
"and you can find out more on `Python packaging user guide <https://packaging."
"python.org/guides/packaging-namespace-packages/>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:522
msgid "``pkg_resource`` style namespace package"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:523
msgid ""
"This is the method ``setuptools`` directly supports. Starting with the same "
"layout, there are two pieces you need to add to it. First, an ``__init__."
"py`` file directly under your namespace package directory that contains the "
"following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:528
msgid "__import__(\"pkg_resources\").declare_namespace(__name__)"
msgstr "__import__(\"pkg_resources\").declare_namespace(__name__)"

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:532
msgid ""
"And the ``namespace_packages`` keyword in your ``setup.cfg`` or ``setup.py``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:536
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"namespace_packages = timmins"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:543
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    # ...\n"
"    namespace_packages=['timmins']\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:550
msgid "And your directory should look like this"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/package_discovery.rst:552
msgid ""
"foo\n"
"├── pyproject.toml  # AND/OR setup.cfg, setup.py\n"
"└── src\n"
"    └── timmins\n"
"        ├── __init__.py\n"
"        └── foo\n"
"            └── __init__.py"
